# Can a price rise make you buy more?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is a Giffen good? What did Jensen and Miller find with rice in China?"
next: {"title":"How do we get from one person to a demand curve?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/78-how-do-we-get-from-one-person-to-a.cast"}

## Stigler's dare
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - rice
      - oil & fat
      - wheat
      - meat
      - veg
      - beans
    values:
      - 64
      - 13
      - 8
      - 7
      - 5
      - 2
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: ' %'
      decimals: 0
    bar_colors: same
    colors:
      - blue
      - grey
      - grey
      - red
      - grey
      - grey
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 75
    y_label: share of calories (%)
    x_label: poor families, Hunan, 2006
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 130
      w: 600
      h: 520
sources:
    - id: stigler1987
      title: The Theory of Price (4th ed.), p. 23
      authors: George J. Stigler
      year: 1987
      finding: Whoever demonstrated a failure of the law of demand 'would be assured of immortality, professionally speaking'.
    - id: rosen1999
      title: Potato Paradoxes
      authors: Sherwin Rosen
      year: 1999
      finding: 'The Irish famine potato is not a Giffen good: the price rose because blight destroyed the crop.'
    - id: jm2008
      title: Giffen Behavior and Subsistence Consumption
      authors: Robert T. Jensen; Nolan H. Miller
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Random rice-price vouchers (up to 0.30 off 1.20 yuan/jin) for 5 months in 2006: poor Hunan households got 64% of calories from rice and 7% from meat; those below 80% rice calorie share ate less rice when it was cheaper (Giffen behaviour).'

    text law "price ↑  →  buy less" x 500 y 560 font_size 44 hidden true
    text quote "\"assured of immortality,\nprofessionally speaking\"" cites ["stigler1987"] x 420 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true
    portrait stigler x 830 y 420 width 190 of "George Stigler" hidden true
    text potato "Irish potatoes, 1840s" #b5482e x 420 y 250 font_size 36 hidden true
    mark x_potato around potato #b5482e kind strike hidden true
    scratch calc cites ["jm2008"] work ["rice: 1.20 yuan/jin (½ kg)","voucher: −0.30","up to 25 % off"] x 790 y 440 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Stigler's dare"

Can a price rise ever make you buy more of something? The law of demand says no: dearer means less.
    draw law

B: George Stigler, a Nobel laureate, wrote that anyone who found a real exception would be
    draw stigler

B: 'assured of immortality, professionally speaking.'
    draw quote

    erase stigler

For decades the textbook exception was Irish potatoes during the famine of the 1840s.
    draw potato

B: Except the price rose because blight des (@draw x_potato@)troyed the crop. Ireland as a whole could hardly have eaten more potatoes.

    erase law quote potato x_potato

So in 2006 two economists, Robert Jensen and Nolan Miller, went looking among some of the poorest families in urban China.
    draw axes bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 bar_6

In Hunan province these families got nearly two thirds of their calories from one food: rice.
    draw bar_1

B: Meat, the food they actually liked, gave them about seven percent.
    highlight bar_4

For five months, randomly chosen families got vouchers
    draw calc_box calc_line_1

that cut the price of rice by up to a quarter.
    draw calc_line_2 calc_line_3

B: Cheaper rice. Surely they ate more of it?
    highlight bar_1

    erase calc

    quiz "Rice got up to a quarter cheaper. What did families who still ate some other food do?" judge false
        right: Here is what the data showed.
        * Ate more rice
        * Ate the same
        * Ate less rice

Among families who got at least a fifth of their calories from other foods, it went the other way: cheaper rice, less rice.
    text res1 "cheaper rice\n→ less rice" #b5482e cites ["jm2008"] x 850 y 470 font_size 36

And when the vouchers ended, rice went back up.
    text res2 "vouchers end\n→ more rice" #2f6b8f x 850 y 330 font_size 36

## Two pulls on the rice bowl
domain: {"x":[0,16],"y":[0,5]}
vars:
    p:
      value: 1
      min: 0.8
      max: 2.4
    r:
      expr: min(30/(6-p), 12/p)
    m:
      expr: (12 - p*r)/3
sources:
    - id: marshall1895
      title: Principles of Economics (3rd ed.)
      authors: Alfred Marshall
      year: 1895
      finding: '''As Mr. Giffen has pointed out, a rise in the price of bread makes so large a drain on the resources of the poorer labouring families ... that they consume more, and not less of it.'''
    - id: jm2008
      title: Giffen Behavior and Subsistence Consumption
      authors: Robert T. Jensen; Nolan H. Miller
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Hunan households eating at least 50 g of meat per person: a 1% rise in the rice price cut meat consumption by about 1.1% (Table 3, col. 9).'

    axes ax x_label "rice (bowls a day)" y_label "meat (portions a day)" cross corner ticks.x [2,4,6,8,10,12] ticks.y [1,2,3,4] hidden true
    curve budget thick #2f6b8f expr "max(0, (12 - p*x)/3)" x_from 0 x_to 12.5 hidden true
    text l_budget "budget: 12 yuan" #2f6b8f at.data [2.6,4.3] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve cal dashed #3d3833 expr "14 - 2*x" x_from 4.75 x_to 7 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    text l_cal "enough calories" at.data [6.9,4.5] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot pick #b5482e at.x 6 at.y 2 bind {"at.x":"r","at.y":"m"} hidden true
    label l_pick "{r:1} rice, {m:1} meat" right #b5482e attach_to pick font_size 28 hidden true
    text subst "substitution: less rice" #2f6b8f x 760 y 520 font_size 30 hidden true
    text incom "income: more rice" #b5482e x 760 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text giffen "Giffen good" #b5482e cites ["marshall1895"] x 760 y 500 font_size 40 hidden true
    text hunan "Hunan: rice +1 %\n→ meat −1.1 %" cites ["jm2008"] x 760 y 500 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch mathcard work [{"tex":"p\\,r + 3m = 12"},{"tex":"2r + m = 14"},{"tex":"r = \\frac{30}{6 - p}"}] x 820 y 430 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    text readout "rice: {p:2} yuan a bowl" #2f6b8f x 790 y 640 font_size 30 hidden true
    curve budget0 #2f6b8f expr "(12 - x)/3" x_from 0 x_to 12 style.opacity 0.3 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    dot pick0 #b5482e at.x 6 at.y 2 style.opacity 0.35 hidden true

    card title "Two pulls on the rice bowl"

B: So cheaper rice meant less rice. How can that be?
    draw ax

Draw one family, with illustrative numbers. Across, bowls of rice a day; up, portions of meat.
    highlight ax

B: Say they have twelve yuan a day for food. Rice costs one yuan a bowl, meat three.
    draw budget l_budget readout

Every point on this line spends all (@point at.data [12,0] gesture tap@) twelve: twelve bowls of r (@point at.data [0,4] gesture tap@)ice, or four portions of meat, or a mix.
    blocking false

B: And this steep line is just enough calories. Anything below it means going hungry.
    draw cal l_cal

Meat tastes better; rice fills you up. So they buy as much meat as they can while still eating (@draw pick l_pick@) enough: right where the lines cross. Six bowls, two portions.

B: Now rice gets dearer, one and a half yuan a bowl.
    highlight readout

    show budget0 pick0

The budget line swings in. To stay fed they must give up meat, and the cheapest way to replace those calories is still rice: about six and two-thirds bowls.
    animate p 1.5 duration 3

B: Two pulls on the bowl, then. Dearer rice says buy less: the substitution effect.
    draw subst

B: Being poorer says buy more of the cheap filler: the income effect.
    draw incom

    erase subst incom

Here the income effect wins, because rice takes so much of the budget. A good like that i (@draw giffen@)s called a Giffen good, after Robert Giffen, whose idea Alfred Marshall reported in 1895.

    erase giffen

B: And in Hunan, families eating a fair amount of meat cut it by about one percent for every one percent rise in the price of rice.
    draw hunan

    draw mathq

    ask "Want the math behind the rice bowl?" on mathq store mq default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq hunan

In symbols: spend it all, p times r plus three m equals twelve.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

Eat enough: two r plus m equals fourteen.
    draw mathcard_line_2

Together, r is thirty over six minus p. A higher price shrinks the bottom, so r grows, until meat hits zero.
    draw mathcard_line_3

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq hunan

Try it: push the price of rice past where the meat runs out, and watch the rice bowl.
    explore params ["p"]

Past about one seventy, there is no meat left to give up. Then dearer rice just means less rice, and hunger.
    animate p 2.2 duration 3

## Poor, but not too poor
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - all families
      - rice < 80 %
      - rice > 80 %
    values:
      - 0.22
      - 0.45
      - -0.61
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      decimals: 2
    colors:
      - grey
      - green
      - red
    ylim:
      - -0.8
      - 0.6
    y_label: '% more rice per 1 % price rise'
    x_label: rice's share of calories · Hunan 2006
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 180
      w: 540
      h: 440
sources:
    - id: jm2008
      title: Giffen Behavior and Subsistence Consumption
      authors: Robert T. Jensen; Nolan H. Miller
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Price elasticity of rice demand in Hunan: all households +0.22 (SE 0.149, not significant); rice under 80% of calories +0.45 (p < 0.01); over 80% −0.61; the 80% cut-off is a rough approximation from a minimum-cost-diet calculation (ISCS 0.79–0.86); cut-offs from 70% to 90% give +0.27 to +0.47; weaker evidence for wheat in Gansu.'
    - id: jm2011
      title: Do Consumer Price Subsidies Really Improve Nutrition? (Review of Economics and Statistics 93(4))
      authors: Robert T. Jensen; Nolan H. Miller
      year: 2011
      finding: 'The same randomized rice/wheat subsidies in Hunan and Gansu did not improve nutrition, and may have reduced it for some households: the wealth effect shifted spending toward tastier, less nutritious foods.'

    text but "But…" #b5482e x 830 y 560 font_size 56 hidden true
    scratch cut cites ["jm2008"] work ["cut-off 70–90 %:","+0.27 to +0.47","Gansu wheat: weaker"] x 830 y 470 font_size 26 hidden true
    text conf1 "Giffen behaviour exists:\nmostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 830 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    text conf2 "Market demand slopes\ndown: consensus" #87a878 x 830 y 440 font_size 28 hidden true
    text beh "Giffen behaviour,\nnot Giffen good" #b5482e x 830 y 300 font_size 30 hidden true
    text nutr "vouchers: calories\nbarely changed" #3d3833 cites ["jm2011"] x 830 y 180 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Poor, but not too poor"

B: So 'buy more when it's dearer' only holds in a band: poor, but not too poor?
    draw axes

That's the 'But…', and the data say so. Jensen and Miller split the Hunan families by how much of their calories came from rice.
    draw but

Where rice was under eighty percent of calories, a one percent price rise meant about half a percent more rice.
    draw bar_2

B: Where it was over eighty, with almost nothing else to cut, rice fell, by about six-tenths of a percent.
    draw bar_3

    erase but

And all the families together: plus 0.22, too uncertain to rule out zero. Pool everyone, and the effect almost disappears.
    draw bar_1

B: Isn't eighty percent a cut-off chosen after seeing the data?
    highlight bar_2 bar_3

Roughly, from a cheapest-healthy-diet calculation, and every cut-off from seventy to ninety still showed it.
    draw cut_box cut_line_1 cut_line_2

Wheat in Gansu showed it only weakly.
    draw cut_line_3

    erase cut

How sure are we? That some very poor families behave this way: mostly agreed, on one careful experiment.
    draw conf1

That market demand curves slope down: consensus.
    draw conf2

B: Which is why they write Giffen behaviour, not Giffen good: it belongs to people in a tight spot, not to rice.
    draw beh

And the vouchers did little for hunger: a la (@draw nutr@)ter paper by the same pair found calories barely changed, as families spent the savings on tastier food.

So yes, a price rise can make you buy more, when that good is your cheapest way not to go hungry and you still have something dearer to give up.
    highlight bar_2

    quiz "Petrol gets 20% dearer. Solveig drives to work and spends about 3% of her budget on petrol. Could the price rise make her buy more petrol?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: No: petrol is a small slice of her budget, so being poorer barely moves her, and the swap away from dearer petrol wins.
        wrong: 3% of a budget can't make a big income effect: here, no.
        * Yes, if she is poor enough
        + No: the income effect is far too small
        * Yes, because she must drive

    quiz "A luxury handbag sells more after its price goes up. Is it a Giffen good?" voice b
        right: No: a dearer handbag doesn't make its buyers so much poorer that they need more of a cheap staple; whatever drives those sales, it isn't the income effect.
        wrong: No cheap staple is being squeezed: here, no.
        * Yes
        + No

    quiz "Eirik, a student, lives mostly on cheap pasta, with a little cheese as a treat. His grant goes up. What happens to how much pasta he eats?"
        right: Less: pasta is his cheap filler, an inferior good for him, so with more money he swaps some of it for tastier food.
        wrong: With more money, he needn't fill up on the cheapest food: here, less.
        * More
        + Less
        * No change

For almost everyone else, dearer still means less.

